# Copy to `.env` and fill in the blanks.

# === Which database this process writes to ====================================
# 'prod' or 'dev'. No default, deliberately. This was `DATABASE_URL`, a bare
# name every tool reached for without deciding anything -- and what it reached
# was production. Every entry point now refuses to start without a target and
# logs which one it resolved.
RISK_DB_TARGET='prod'

# === PostgreSQL (Neon) — PRODUCTION ===========================================
# postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:PORT/DBNAME?sslmode=require
# The harvest cron feeds this one. Ad-hoc queries: prod_readonly() in
# backend/util/db.py, which cannot write.
#
# Was `DATABASE_URL`. The frontend also reads a variable of that name, but from
# its own frontend/.env in its own hosting environment -- a different process
# and never the same variable, whatever the shared spelling suggested.
PROD_DATABASE_URL=''

# === PostgreSQL (Neon) — DEVELOPMENT ==========================================
# Second, separate project: the experiment store, where the A/B arms and the
# scored reference live. dev() in backend/util/db.py.
# Check both strings:  python -m backend.util.db
DEV_DATABASE_URL=''

# === OpenAI ===================================================================
# Do NOT expose via NEXT_PUBLIC_*.
OPENAI_API_KEY=''

# === Scorer override (bake-off only; leave every line unset to run production)==
# `backend/llm/client.py` reads a quad per stage. Setting any of these moves the
# scorer, so `bakeoff.candidate_env` clears all of them between candidates --
# one model's payload scored at another model's endpoint, filed under the wrong
# name, is the failure the clearing exists to prevent.
#
#   SCORING_MODEL       the model id sent, e.g. gpt-4.1-2025-04-14
#   SCORING_BASE_URL    an OpenAI-compatible endpoint. Unset means api.openai.com
#   SCORING_API_KEY     the key for that endpoint, if it is not the OpenAI one
#   SCORING_EXTRA_BODY  JSON merged into the request body, e.g.
#                       '{"reasoning_effort": "none"}'. Malformed JSON raises
#                       rather than being dropped -- a failed reasoning pin
#                       costs money silently.
#
# For a locally served model (llama.cpp, vLLM, Ollama), point BASE_URL at the
# server and give it any non-empty key; the client insists on one and the server
# ignores it. `docs/scorer-acceptance.md` has the worked example, and the
# `local-template` entry in `bakeoff.CANDIDATES` is the same thing in code.
# SCORING_MODEL=''
# SCORING_BASE_URL='http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1'
# SCORING_LOCAL_KEY='not-a-real-key'
#
# `DIGEST_*` is the same quad for the stage-1 article digest. Note that
# `build_digest_chat` -- the masking instrument, i.e. rewrite, sweep, probe and
# leakage scan -- is pinned and honours no override at all, deliberately.

# === Guardian Content API =====================================================
# Free key: https://open-platform.theguardian.com/access/
# History Machine harvest only; the daily ETL never reads it.
GUARDIAN_API_KEY=''

# === New York Times Archive API ===============================================
# Free key: https://developer.nytimes.com/ — create an app, enable "Archive API".
# History Machine only. One call = one month; 5/min, 500/day.
NYT_API_KEY=''

# === Crawlbase ================================================================
# Server-side token for the REST API; the JS one is optional, client-side.
CRAWLBASE_TOKEN=''
CRAWLBASE_JS_TOKEN=''

# === FMP (Financial Modeling Prep) ============================================
# Econ calendar + prices (util/prices.py). Plan must include those endpoints.
FMP_API_KEY=''
